   delivered pressure ordered by the ceiling:      15 / 15 concordant pairs
   cycle time ordered by the S6>S7 driving force:  15 / 15 concordant pairs (shorter as drive rises)
   The ratio-chain product is what a model of independent stages predicts;
   it overshoots the paper by 2.5 to 4.6 orders of magnitude. The cascade is set by
   its last stage. Note paper/ceiling is NOT monotone (0.798 at 110 C, 0.767 at 120 C):
   the ceiling orders the cases, it does not predict the kinetic gap.

D. An open question for the authors (not a claim)
   The text reports 180 bar (80 C) and 365 bar (120 C) at the third coupling.
   Coupling 1 is S1 -> S2 by the paper's own Step 3, so the third is S3 -> S4.
   Flat-plateau brackets [P_abs,i+1(10 C), P_des,i(T_hot)] that admit each value:
   180 bar @ 80 C:  S4>S5  S5>S6
   365 bar @ 120 C:  S4>S5  S5>S6  S6>S7
   S3 desorbs at 101.46 bar at 80 C, below 180 bar: either the figure counts couplings differently,
   or the S3 plateau slope is large. Only the authors' sigma_s, sigma_0 and Y settle it.

E. Why the van 't Hoff covariance matters (the next data request)
   A +-1 kJ/mol Delta H(des) at fixed Delta S moves the 80 C ceiling by a factor 1.406
   The same shift, compensated at an illustrative 60 C pivot, moves it by 1.021
   Independent intervals on Delta H and Delta S overstate the ceiling's
   uncertainty by an order of magnitude in log space. The covariance of each
   van 't Hoff fit is the single number per stage that would close this.

